    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "88e1fb42_2bbcc9a8",
        "filename": "chip/rotor/hwtimer.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 89,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000187
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-24T19:29:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Both of these do terrible things the way common/timer.c is currently implemented (we have the same problem in chip/g)\n\nOther parts of the code assume this returns a full-range 32-bit value, not something that wraps before 0xffffffff.\n\nSo, say you wrap at 0x0aaaaaaa because CLOCK_FREQ is 24 MHz.  If you start udelay(10000) at 0x0aaaaaa0, then 10 ticks later wrap to 0x00000000, udelay will think that roughly 4000 seconds have passed and will exit.\n\nWe could make __hw_clock_source_read keep its own 32-bit accumulator.  But it\u0027s probably better just to add support to common/timer.c to handle the case where __hw_clock_source_read() returns something not microseconds (could be a 24MHz clock, could be a 262KHz clock), because we\u0027ve run into this on several chips now.  And then make sure that ONLY timer.c calls __hw_clock_source_read().",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 77,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 89,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "8c1c051bdcb6792030e4557560403e63f88d0cbb",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
